      "body": "`c2go` incorrectly translates C increment/decrement expressions used inside larger expressions into Go compound assignments.\nIn C, `++x`, `x++`, `--x`, and `x--` are expressions, so they can appear in function arguments, unary expressions, arithmetic expressions, and other expression contexts. In Go, compound assignments such as `x += 1` and `x -= 1` are statements, so they cannot appear inside another expression. This produces syntactically invalid Go code.\n**To Reproduce**\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", ++x);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n**Generated Go Code**\n```go\nfunc main() {\n        var x int32 = int32(1)\n        noarch.Printf((&[]byte(\"%d\\n\\x00\")[0]), x += 1)\n        return\n}\n```\n**Observed Behavior**\nThe generated Go code fails to compile:\n```\n# command-line-arguments\n./test.go:164:44: syntax error: unexpected += in argument list; possibly missing comma or )\n```\n**Additional Example**\nThe same issue can also occur in other expression contexts, for example:\n```\nint complex = !(a++) + !(--b);\n```\nwhich is translated into invalid Go code like:\n```\nvar complex int32 = noarch.NotInt32((a += 1)) + noarch.NotInt32((b -= 1))\n```\nThis fails because `a += 1` and `b -= 1` cannot be used as expressions in Go.",
